    "lawCode": "Amd §8007, CPLR; amd §70-a, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4796\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the civil practice law and rules and\nthe public officers law, in relation to fees allowed to be charged for\ncertain advertising\n\n PURPOSE : The purpose of this legislation is to provide equity in the\nfees that are charged for certain public advertising between the New\nYork City and metropolitan daily newspapers. It would allow that\ncertain newspapers in Richmond County may charge the regularly\nestablished classified advertising rate for publication of legal\nnotices.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\n§1 - amends Section 8007 of the civil practice and rules to include\nnewspapers principally printed, circulated or having its principal\noffice in Richmond county within the language exempting newspapers\npublished in the city of New York and several other counties from the\nstatutory rate set forth in the section.\n\n§2 - amends section 70-a of the public officers law to include\nnewspapers principally printed, circulated or having its principal\noffice in Richmond county within the language exempting newspapers\npublished in the city of New York and several other counties from the\nstatutory rate set forth in the section.\n\n§3 - This act shall take effect immediately\n\n EXISTING LAW : Currently only the following counties permitted to\ncharge regularly established classified advertising rates for the\npublication of legal notices: New York, Bronx, Kings, Queens, Nassau,\nWestchester and the City of Buffalo.\n\n JUSTIFICATION : Newspapers published in all counties in the New York\nMetropolitan area (the \"Area\") other than Richmond County have been\nexempted from the statutory fee that is set forth in the two sections\nof the law involved. The exemptions were granted due to the high costs\nof labor, goods and services in the Area. Newspapers in Richmond\nCounty face the same high costs as the other newspapers in the Area.\nMoreover, these costs have dramatically increased since the law was\nlast amended to add Nassau County. It is now difficult, if not\nimpossible, to cover the expense of publishing the notices that are\nrequired to be published in a newspaper by law or court order by\ncharging the statutory fee as opposed to established classified\nadvertising rates. This bill would help ensure the continued\npublication of these types of notices to the citizens of Richmond\nCounty who rely on newspapers published in that county.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY : 2009 Session: New Legislation\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : Slight increase in the cost of publication of\ncertain state and local government notices as required by law or court\norder.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE : Immediately\n",
